  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "Socioeconomic drivers were evaluated using a two-step model at the prefecture level, which may smooth out finer neighborhood-scale dynamics.",
      "what": "Using a buffer-overlap method at 30-meter resolution across five-year steps from 2000 to 2020, this study mapped China's wildland-urban interface (WUI) and classified it by vegetation types: forest, shrub, and grassland. The national WUI expanded by 22.99% over two decades to reach 528,000 square kilometers (5.50% of China's land area), driven primarily by urban growth. Fire activity and human exposure concentrated heavily in the 0-1 km buffer surrounding the designated WUI boundaries.",
      "why": "By demonstrating that fire activity and population exposure peak immediately outside defined WUI boundaries rather than inside them, the results challenge conventional spatial targeting for fire management and planning."
    }
